"There are three different reports regarding the occasions of revelation (asbab al-nuzul) for the opening verses of Surah al-Ankabut. One of these concerns Ammar ibn Yasir and his companions being tortured by the polytheists in Mecca. According to the second narration, the Muslims in Medina wrote to those in Mecca who had promised to embrace Islam that they must emigrate (hijrah); they set out, but the polytheists hindered them. The third report concerns Mihja' ibn Abdullah, who was martyred in the Battle of Badr. Regarding the expression that Allah 'will surely know,' it is emphasized that His knowledge does not change, but rather what is known changes. The purpose is to distinguish the believers from the liars through testing and to recompense them.\n\nLet those who commit evil deeds not assume that they can outstrip Allah and escape. These evil deeds refer particularly to the oppression and torment inflicted upon the believers. Those who hope to meet Allah must persevere with patience until the time He has appointed and perform righteous deeds. Allah is the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing; He hears prayers and knows intentions.\n\nWhoever strives (engages in jihad) does so only for his own benefit, because the reward of this effort belongs to himself, not to Allah. Allah is free of all needs from anything. It is probable that these verses were revealed in Medina, because the hypocrites were there. The believers have faith that there is no god but Allah, and they praise Him.\n\nOn the Day of Resurrection, everyone will bear their own sins, and they will also carry the sins of those whom they led astray. Noah (peace be upon him) remained among his people for nine hundred and fifty years, and ultimately they were destroyed by the flood. Noah and those aboard the ark were saved, and this event was made a lesson for all the worlds. Seeking protection from Allah's punishment through material means is also by His command.\n\nAbraham (peace be upon him) called his people to worship Allah and stated that worshiping idols is a falsehood. Idols cannot provide sustenance; sustenance is with Allah. Abraham was saved from the fire by Allah against his people's threat to kill or burn him. Subsequently, Lot believed in him, and Abraham emigrated to his Lord. Allah granted him Isaac and Jacob, and bestowed prophethood and the scriptures upon his descendants.\n\nLot (peace be upon him) forbade his people from their indecencies, such as approaching men, highway robbery, and committing obscenities in their gatherings, but they demanded Allah's punishment instead. The messengers gave Abraham the tidings that the people of Lot would be destroyed; Lot and his family were saved, while his wife remained behind in the punishment. This event is a clear lesson for those who use their intellect.\n\nShu'ayb (peace be upon him) invited the people of Midian to worship Allah, to hope for the Last Day, and forbade them from causing corruption. When they rejected him, they were destroyed by a tremor. The peoples of 'Ad and Thamud were also destroyed, for Satan had made their deeds seem pleasing to them. Korah (Qarun), Pharaoh, and Haman also acted arrogantly despite the clear proofs brought by Moses, and each was seized by a different form of punishment.\n\nThe parable of those who take protectors (awliya) other than Allah is like that of a spider that builds a web. Just as the spider's web is frail, so too is what they rely upon frail. This parable also encompasses hidden polytheism (shirk); those who perform worship with ostentation (riya) have also taken a protector other than Allah. Allah knows whatever things are invoked besides Him. He is the All-Mighty, the All-Wise. These parables are understood only by those possessed of knowledge. The heavens and the earth were created in truth; in this there is indeed a sign for the believers.\n\nRecite what has been revealed to you of the Book and establish the prayer. Indeed, prayer restrains from shameful and evil deeds. The remembrance of Allah is the greatest act of worship. Fulfilling the prayer properly is achieved by observing its boundaries; otherwise, it distances one from Allah. Dispute with the People of the Book in the fairest manner, yet be firm against those who do wrong. Say to them, 'We believe in that which was revealed to us and in that which was revealed to you, and our God is One.' The Quran is attested to by most of those who were given the Scripture. Because you are unlettered (ummi), this Book is a miracle. None reject Our verses except the wrongdoers.\n\nIn verses 50–51, it is explained that the wrongdoers and the People of the Book deny that the Quran is a miracle and demand other miracles from Allah. In response to them, it is emphasized that all miracles are with Allah and that the Messenger of Allah is only a clear warner. Then, it is stated that this Book revealed to an unlettered prophet is, on its own, a sufficient miracle. As the occasion of revelation for these verses, it is narrated that when some Muslims wrote down and brought things they had heard from the Jews, the Prophet warned them and this verse was revealed. However, it is noted that this narration does not entirely fit the context of the verse, because the verse addresses the disbelievers.\n\nIn verses 52–55, it is stated that Allah is sufficient as a witness between the Prophet and the disbelievers, and that He knows everything in the heavens and the earth. It is announced that those who believe in falsehood and disbelieve will be the losers. Then, the disbelievers' haste in demanding punishment is criticized, and it is stated that were it not for an appointed term, the punishment would have come immediately; however, it will surely strike them suddenly while they do not perceive it. It is described that Hell will encompass the disbelievers, that on that Day the punishment will envelop them from every side, and that Allah will say, 'Taste the consequence of what you used to do.'\n\nVerse 56 reads: \"O My servants who have believed! Surely My earth is spacious, so worship only Me.\" The expression \"'ibadi\" (My servants) mentioned here is an address of honor and does not encompass the disbelievers. The verse points out the path of emigration (hijrah) to the believers who cannot practice their religion properly under oppression. That is to say, believers who find themselves in hardship and distress should migrate to another place and continue to worship Allah alone, making every sacrifice for this cause.\n\nIn verses 57–59, it is recalled that every soul will taste death and will ultimately be returned to Allah. Those who believe and do righteous deeds are promised mansions in Paradise, wherein they shall abide eternally. These are the ones who are patient and rely only upon their Lord. These verses emphasize that those who emigrate should not step back out of fear of death, because death is inevitable everywhere and what truly matters is sincere devotion to Allah.\n\nIn verses 60–63, it is stated that Allah provides the sustenance of many creatures and human beings that do not carry their own provision. It is noted that if the polytheists were asked who created the heavens and the earth and subjected the sun and the moon, they would surely say, \"Allah.\" Likewise, they acknowledge that it is Allah who sends down water from the sky and revives the earth after its death. Despite this, it is questioned how they turn away from the truth and stray into polytheism (shirk). It is recalled that Allah extends provision abundantly for whom He wills and restricts it for whom He wills. These verses teach that those who emigrate should not be overwhelmed by worries over livelihood, and that they must trust that provision comes solely from Allah.\n\nIn verses 64–69, it is stated that the worldly life is nothing but diversion and play, and that the true life is the abode of the Hereafter. It is recounted that when people board a ship, they call upon Allah with sincere devotion, but as soon as they reach dry land, they revert to polytheism. It is mentioned that although Mecca was made a secure sanctuary, people believe in falsehood and show ingratitude. It is declared that none is more unjust than one who invents a lie against Allah or denies the truth, and that Hell is the abode for the disbelievers. Finally, the glad tidings are given that Allah will guide those who strive in His cause to His ways, and that He is with those who do good."